# Algorithms

One card per sort. Each states the invariant it maintains, the flow it follows,
and what its cost is made of.

- [[bubble-sort]] — repeatedly swap adjacent inversions
- [[insertion-sort]] — grow a sorted prefix, one element at a time
- [[merge-sort]] — split, sort both halves, merge
- [[quicksort]] — partition around a pivot, recurse on both sides
- [[heapsort]] — build a heap, then extract the maximum repeatedly

The cost model they are all measured against is [[complexity]], and the property
that most often decides between them is [[stability]].

## The two families

The first two maintain a **sorted region** that grows by one element per pass.
The last three **divide** the problem, which is what buys the $\log n$ factor:

![[complexity#^lower-bound]]
